---
import BaseLayout from "~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const lang = "en" as const;
const date = "2026-05-02";
const title = "Why I file advancement misfires as S1, not S3";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, "");
const langPrefix = `${base}/en`;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} lang={lang}>
  <article class="note">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="note__head">
        <p class="eyebrow"><strong>note</strong> · <span class="mono tnum">{date}</span></p>
        <h1 class="note__title h-display">{title}</h1>
        <p class="note__deck">
          An advancement firing at the wrong moment looks like a small cosmetic bug. It isn't — it lies to the player. The system says "you did X" when the player didn't. That's an integrity bug, not polish.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="note__body">
        <h2>The S3 temptation</h2>
        <p>
          On its surface, "an advancement popped at the wrong time" reads like S3: nothing crashed, nothing locked up, no item was lost, no save was corrupted. The player can keep playing the game and never feel the impact. The first time I saw one I wanted to file it as S3 — drop it into the polish bucket and let the author fix it on a quiet weekend.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then I logged this entry against <a href={`${langPrefix}/cases/alex_caves`}>Alex's Caves</a>:
          <em>"Single-player. A new save auto-unlocks the advancement (Where did the gummies go?) the moment you log in."</em>
          New save, fresh login, the player hasn't moved a pixel — and the advancement has already fired.
        </p>

        <h2>What it lies about</h2>
        <p>
          Minecraft's advancement system isn't decoration. It's the game's official record of what the player has done:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Players use it to decide what to do next</li>
          <li>Mod authors use it as a quest gate (gating subsequent recipes / items / regions)</li>
          <li>When players say in Discord / forums "I just hit advancement X", they're treating the system as a fair referee</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A prematurely-fired advancement breaks all three: the player is steered toward a meaningless next step, the quest gate opens for the wrong reason, and community sharing turns into untrustworthy boasting. It isn't a broken decoration — it's a referee raising one team's victory flag before the match has started.
        </p>

        <h2>The rubric</h2>
        <p>
          So in my own rubric, advancement misfires explicitly belong to S1:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>S1</strong>: auto-unlock on a new save; should-fire-but-doesn't; fires on the wrong target (kill A and B's advancement triggers)</li>
          <li><strong>S2</strong>: trigger condition slightly off (you had to do it 6 times when the doc says 5)</li>
          <li><strong>S3</strong>: typo in the displayed text; wrong icon color</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The question isn't "what did this bug block?" — it's "what untrue thing did this bug make the system say?".
          Blocker bugs go to S0; lying bugs go to S1. Neither can wait for polish week.
        </p>

        <h2>The actual effect on mod authors</h2>
        <p>
          The practical consequence of filing advancement bugs as S1 is that when the author reads my report, the advancement batch sits at the top alongside the crashes.
          They get fixed in the first regression cycle, not pushed to "maybe next version".
          Across Hot Bath's 4 regression rounds, this rubric let advancement-class bugs get fixed roughly twice as fast as spec-mismatch bugs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Not because they're harder to fix — quite the opposite, advancement bugs are usually a JSON predicate typo or a missed trigger condition —
          but because <strong>the S1 tag forces the author to re-rank priority for me</strong>.
          I don't have to argue. The rubric does the arguing.
        </p>

        <h2>The portable rule</h2>
        <p>
          If you're testing mods too, the next time you see an advancement firing at the wrong time, ask yourself one question:
          <strong>what untrue thing is this bug making the game say to the player?</strong>
          If the answer is "nothing" (e.g. a pure typo), it's S3. If the answer is "it misrepresents the player's state, progress, or eligibility", it's S1.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rule generalizes well past advancements — quest systems, reputation systems, unlock systems, even buff timer displays.
          Anywhere the game makes a <em>promise</em> to the player, a broken promise is S1, not polish.
        </p>
      </div>

      <footer class="note__foot">
        <p class="note__back">
          <a href={`${langPrefix}/notes`}>← back to notes</a>
          <span class="note__sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <a href={`${langPrefix}/methodology#achievement`}>see the achievement test method</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .note { padding: 2.4rem 0 4rem 0; }
  .note__head {
    max-width: 64ch;
    padding-bottom: 1.4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .note__title {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem 0;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .note__deck {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .note__body { max-width: 64ch; }
  .note__body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    margin: 2.2rem 0 0.85rem 0;
    color: var(--ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }
  .note__body p, .note__body ul {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem 0;
  }
  .note__body ul { padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .note__body li { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .note__body strong { color: var(--accent-deep); font-weight: 600; }
  .note__body em { color: var(--ink-soft); font-style: normal; background: var(--bg-soft); padding: 0.05em 0.3em; border-radius: 3px; }
  .note__body a {
    color: var(--ink);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .note__body a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .note__foot {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .note__back {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink-mute);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .note__back a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  .note__back a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); }
  .note__sep { margin: 0 0.5rem; }
</style>
